    <rule id="2fda-64e6-df1e-499c" name="Late Roman (Armies of Antiquity) - List Notes" publicationId="aab0-aa1e-0319-4ffe" hidden="false">
      <description>LATE ROMAN ARMY LIST (Armies of Antiquity, pp.24-25), AD 285-475.
Characters: the army may have a General and an Army Battle Standard. Cavalry up to three quarters of the points; Infantry up to three quarters; Barbarian Allies up to half; one artillery piece per 500 points in the army.
Guard Troops: "Guard" marks any top-quality unit. The Craig Davey Option (optional): count Guard units with the characteristics of the ordinary troops in the entry below them, but drilled and stubborn, at the same points cost. Not built; use the ordinary entry and add the rules by hand.
Shield Wall: heavy infantry may form a shield wall instead of moving; all enemy attacks from the front (shooting and hand-to-hand) suffer -1 to hit while the unit has not moved or lost a round of close combat.
Combined Formations: heavy and auxiliary infantry may be combined with archers into one regiment, the archers in the rear ranks, losing light infantry status; casualties come from the infantry models first. Not built as a combined unit (field the archers separately).
Barbarian Allies (not built here: make a separate small roster): numeri, symmachiarii or foederati from the Barbarian and Nomadic Hordes lists, up to half the points.

ARMY COMPOSITION (percentages by points):
  Cavalry: up to 75%
  Infantry: up to 75%</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
